   "CHARACTER SHEET - BadgerSnow   (bake FIRST, before any scene)\n\nSame make as the book: fine steel-nib line on cool off-white paper #F4F1EA with ONE flat fill,\nforest black-green #1F2A24. 🔴 Every white part of this animal is BARE PAPER left unfilled -\nno white ink is used on it anywhere. Do not shade, do not render it round.\n\nFACE: badger cub, long low muzzle. The head is flat black fill with the classic mask LEFT AS\n  BARE PAPER: a broad white blaze up the middle of the face and a white band over each cheek and\n  ear, so two black stripes run back through the eyes. The eye is a small reserved paper shape\n  with a black dot in it and a SEPARATE nib brow above - the brows do the acting and must lift,\n  pull together and tilt unevenly. Nose one small filled wedge, whiskers 3 nib strokes a side.\nBODY: low, wide, short-legged, four heads long, blunt stub tail, small round ears. BLACK\n  FOREPAWS, and a pale reserved-paper belly whose fur is drawn only as nib hatching along its\n  edge - the belly matters because the whole book starts with a paw pushed into it.\n🔴 WINTER COAT LADDER (two figures side by side on the sheet): THIN - the belly fur shallow, a\n  paw sunk in only to the back of the hand. THICK - the belly fur visibly fuller and deeper, the\n  same paw sunk in to the wrist. Same pose, same size, so only the depth changes.\n🔴 THE DEMONSTRATION: draw one more head close-up with three tiny six-pointed shapes RESERVED IN\n  BARE PAPER sitting on the black of its brow and shoulder. This is the proof that white shows\n  on black, and the last page needs it.\nSHEET: sitting up with one forepaw pushed into the belly fur / sitting with the head thrown right\n  back and the throat exposed, looking straight up / lying back against a tree base with the hind\n  legs stretched out / lying forward with the muzzle almost on the ground looking down; plus three\n  close-ups - earnest and concentrating, mouth wide open shouting with delight, and eyes screwed\n  up with one paw pressing a stiff neck. Plain paper, no forest. SCENE token: BadgerSnow.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - SquirrelKid   (bake SECOND)\n\nSame make and same nib as BadgerSnow - attach the approved BadgerSnow sheet so the two are drawn\nby one hand. This animal is ONE FLAT FILL, orange #D4642A, and it is the only warm colour in the\nentire book. It is never shaded, never tinted, never given a highlight.\n\nFACE: young red squirrel, short muzzle, big dark eye as a small reserved paper shape with a black\n  dot, a SEPARATE nib brow above each. Long EAR TUFTS in the same orange fill, drawn as a few nib\n  points at the edge - the tufts are the silhouette and must read at thumbnail size.\nBODY: small, four heads tall, big bushy tail that curls up over the back. Belly fur a little\n  paler by leaving a nib-hatched reserved area, never by mixing a second orange.\n🔴 SCALE PLATE: draw the squirrel beside BadgerSnow at THE SAME DEPTH - the badger is FOUR TO\n  FIVE TIMES its size, and one badger head is about as big as the whole squirrel's body. Two\n  pages of the book put them nose to nose and perspective must not flip that.\nSHEET: crouched on all fours with one forepaw pushed down through a hole / standing up on the hind\n  legs with both forepaws thrown high and the tail fanned / standing on the hind legs with the\n  neck stretched right up, chin high / sitting pressed against a badger's flank with the tail\n  wrapped round itself / lying forward with the muzzle down beside a badger's muzzle; plus two\n  close-ups - triumphant with the mouth wide, and blinking slowly with the tufts laid back.\n  🔴 WET PAW: one small study of a forepaw soaked to the wrist, drawn with nib lines only and a\n  single drop - no shine, no white. Plain paper. SCENE token: SquirrelKid.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - GladeSet   (bake THIRD - the stage, used on every page)\n\nThe clearing, empty of animals, exactly as it sits on all twelve pages. Fine nib line, the two\nflat fills, and bare paper. Nothing else exists in this book.\n\nTHE PLACE: one big spruce, its base filling the left of the space, with a BLACK BURROW MOUTH at\n  its foot - one flat #1F2A24 shape with 0 interior, low enough that a badger has to drop a\n  shoulder to get in. TWO PACES in front of it, one small FROZEN PUDDLE, also one flat #1F2A24\n  shape. All around, a wall of spruce as a single black silhouette with at most 9 branch notches\n  on its edge, 0 needles and 0 trunks drawn inside it. Underfoot, wet moss and fallen leaves as\n  NIB HATCHING ONLY, at most 30 short strokes for a whole foreground, 0 outlined leaves. Above,\n  bare paper: 0 marks, ever.\n🔴 THE PUDDLE IS THE ONLY PLACE ANYTHING SHOWS - draw it three times on this sheet:\n  A · thin new ice, a paw broken through it, a star of cracks radiating out and black water\n    welling up, two or three thin plates floating; sunken leaves as LARGE irregular RESERVED\n    PAPER shapes with two vein lines each.\n  B · frozen hard and whole, the old crack line only a faint ghost, nothing on it yet.\n  C · frozen hard with TINY SIX-POINTED SHAPES RESERVED IN BARE PAPER sitting on it, all the\n    same small size and all clearly stars. 🔴 Put B and C side by side and check that a flake\n    can never be mistaken for a sunken leaf - the leaf is big and irregular, the flake is tiny\n    and identical to its neighbours.\nSCALE: the puddle is just big enough that a crouched badger would fill it, and to a squirrel it\n  is a yard - show a badger outline and a squirrel outline beside it at the same depth.\n🔴 NO SNOW LIES ON THE GROUND ON ANY PAGE. 0 lettering, 0 numerals, 0 markers, 0 name tags.\nSCENE token: GladeSet."
