   "CHARACTER SHEET - FourFinds   (bake FIRST, before anything else - this book is these four)\n\nThe four objects the storm left. In the finished book each of these is a REAL CUT PIECE OF PAPER\nglued onto coarse grey millboard #9A9A93 - a visible cut or torn edge, a thin hard shadow under\nthat edge, a slight lift at one corner. Lay all four on plain board AT CORRECT RELATIVE SCALE.\nEach keeps this exact size and shape on every page of the book.\n\n1 GRASS TUFT: a fist-sized bundle of dried grass #C2B07C, cut as one ragged silhouette with\n  loose ends breaking the outline. 🔴 BUFF MOTTLED DOWN #C9B48E is tangled all through it -\n  clumps of it, unmistakably the same colour and the same mottling as the gull's breast.\n2 ROPE END: a short length of pale twisted rope #B9A184, one end frayed into strands, and at the\n  OTHER end a run of small scalloped notches bitten out of it. 🔴 The notches are exactly as wide\n  as a young gull's beak and as deep - a beak tip must be able to sit right into them.\n3 FLAT STONE: a rounded flat stone #7E7F79 cut as one clean shape. 🔴 On its UNDERSIDE, a ring of\n  dried moss stuck round the rim in a circle. Show it from above and from below.\n4 NACRE SCRAP: a thumb-sized shard, the ONE iridescent thing in the book - the front shifts\n  pale green, pink and blue. 🔴 Its BACK is dull and dirty, and dried grass stems plus one strand\n  of buff down are stuck to it hard - the same grass as piece 1. Show front and back side by side.\nPAIRING PLATE: the flat stone's underside laid beside a ring of scraped-bare moss on the ledge\n  rock, the two circles the same size; and the rope's notched end beside a young gull's beak.\nPlain board, no scenery, no animals, no text or numerals. SCENE token: FourFinds.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - CatLighthouse   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\nPainted straight onto coarse grey millboard #9A9A93 in flat opaque paint with a dry ragged edge -\nNOT a cut piece. It must look made of different stuff from the four finds.\n\nBODY: a small black cat #221F22 as one flat shape, no rendered fur, no sheen, no rim light. 🔴\n  WHITE PAWS AND CHEST #FAF8F3 - the four socks and the chest bib are the brightest things on\n  the animal and the only place where what it is carrying can be counted. Tail as one flat\n  tapering shape. Whiskers = 3 thin strokes a side.\nFACE: one round eye with a SEPARATE short brow mark above it - the brows do the acting and must\n  lift, pull together and tilt. Nose = one small wedge, mouth = one line. No catchlight, no\n  drawn fur, no dot-eye without a brow.\nFIXED GRADE: on four legs, and it sits back or rises on its hind legs ONLY while its forepaws\n  are carrying or placing something. It never walks upright and it never uses fingers - the\n  paws hold things pressed flat between them, or scoop them from underneath.\nSHEET: four-legged from the side, one white forepaw lifted / sitting back on the haunches with\n  both white forepaws held out flat together as a shelf / standing wobbling on the hind legs,\n  body tipped, chin pushed down to pin something against the chest / stretching with all four\n  legs planted, forepaws thrown far forward, back arched, tail straight up and quivering; plus\n  three close-ups - startled (brows high, eye round), careful (eyes narrowed, brows level),\n  reluctant (one ear tipped sideways, toes half curled).\nPlain board, no scenery, no objects in the paws. SCENE token: CatLighthouse.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - GullYoung   (bake THIRD, attach as @image3)\n\nSame make as the cat: painted flat onto the board, not cut paper.\n\nBODY: a young herring gull, still in juvenile plumage - BUFF MOTTLED #C9B48E all over the breast\n  and back, 🔴 the same colour and mottling as the down tangled in the grass tuft (piece 1). Legs\n  two pale strokes, webbed feet flat. Beak dark-tipped, blunt, and its tip is exactly the width\n  of the notches on the rope end (piece 2).\nFACE: one round dark eye with a SEPARATE short brow mark above it. No catchlight.\nFIXED GRADE: a bird throughout. It grips with the beak, stretches its neck, steps, shuffles and\n  settles. IT NEVER GESTURES WITH A WING, never points, never holds anything with a wing. Wings\n  open only to shake or to balance, and it does not fly in this book.\nSCALE PLATE: GullYoung beside CatLighthouse AT THE SAME DEPTH - the cat is about one head taller.\n  Perspective must never flip these two sizes on any page.\nSHEET: standing on both feet, one wing half shaken out, neck low, looking down / neck stretched\n  far forward, beak tip pressed into a notch / stepping backward, head tipped to watch something\n  being placed / settled down into a nest, breast flat, neck folded into the shoulders, eyes\n  half shut; plus one close-up of the head with the beak parted.\nPlain board, no scenery. SCENE token: GullYoung."
