  "anchor": "STYLE ANCHOR - changjak-riverpour   (young otter and kingfisher / one bend of the Danube)\n\nStyle: poured watercolour on warm oatmeal rough paper #E8DCC4, the board tilted while wet so the\n  colour runs downhill and does the drawing itself. NO LINE IS DRAWN ANYWHERE IN THIS BOOK.\n  Ages 4-6.\n\nRENDERING (finish hierarchy - the one rule): thin muddy colour is poured in at the TOP of the\n  sheet and runs down. Where the flow bit back into a wash it left a hard dark rim - THAT RIM IS\n  THE CLIFF on the left. Where it slowed and dried it dropped sediment as a pale grainy bloom\n  with a crisp tideline - THAT BLOOM IS THE SANDBAR on the right. Every edge is a dried\n  wash edge, a backrun or a bloom; no contour is made with a brush point or a pen. Animals are\n  2-3 poured shapes each with bare paper left inside for the light. THE POUR BEGINS ABOVE THE TOP\n  OF THE FRAME AND IS NEVER SHOWN - upstream is where the paint came from, never a place we see.\n  Depth = thinner, paler pours, never blur, never mist.\n\nPALETTE: paper #E8DCC4 - river #7A5F3C - deep channel #2E2519, smooth and unbroken - cliff ochre\n  #B08A4E - roots #EFE7D2 - dry sand #DCCBA4 - wet sand #A98F62 - willow #6E7A55 - otter #6A5442\n  - ball weed #37502F and under it FADED GOLD #C9A961. KINGFISHER BLUE #2C6FA8 and BELLY ORANGE\n  #D2792F are the only saturated colours here; they belong to that bird and appear nowhere else.\n  No red, no pink, no sunset, no blue sky.\n\nCOUNTS (upper limits - count them): water = poured flow only, at most 5 foam streaks, 0 drawn\n  ripples - sand = 1 bloom plus at most 3 tidelines, 0 grains - cliff = 1 hard rim plus at most 4\n  sediment bands and 3 root shapes - willow = 6 poured strokes a tree, 0 leaves - fur = 0 hairs -\n  drift = exactly the number named in that page's BEND line.\n  FINISHED THINGS PER PAGE = 2 (the otter plus the one thing it holds or reaches for).\n  DENSITY RATION = page 2 only, where the morning's finds lie in a row and finish rises to 5.\n  Extras: 0 boats, 0 people, 0 fences, 0 buildings, 0 second animals.\n\nCHARACTER DESIGN LANGUAGE: bodies are poured silhouettes, readable from shape alone at thumbnail\n  size; each eye is a GAP OF BARE PAPER inside the dark shape, with a separate short dark shape\n  above it for a brow so the otter can act. FIXED GRADE: the otter is on four legs and rises on\n  its haunches only while its forepaws work; it carries nothing in a hand and never shades its\n  eyes like a person - THE BALL IS ALWAYS CLAMPED IN ONE ARMPIT so both forepaws stay free. The\n  kingfisher is a bird: it perches, hops and turns its beak, and NEVER gestures with a wing. No\n  clothes, no fingers, no shoes.\n\nCANVAS: 16:9 double-page spread, one scene, the bottom 18% left quiet for a caption. No text,\n  letters, numerals or watermark anywhere - no signpost, mooring post or marked gear.\n\nNOT: no digital slickness of any kind - airbrush, gradient, glow, 3D CG, cel-shading,\n  photographic, or a paper-texture filter over flat colour (the tilt and the drying must MAKE\n  the edges) / NO DRAWN CONTOUR LINE anywhere, not even on the animals / no mist, fog or haze\n  and nothing out of focus - a backrun has a hard rim / not felt, stitching or clay.",
