   "SET SHEET - RiverBend   (bake FIRST, before anything else - all fifteen pages live inside it)\n\nThe one bend of the river this book never leaves, made entirely of poured watercolour on warm\noatmeal rough paper #E8DCC4. No drawn line anywhere.\n\nLEFT BANK, THE OUTSIDE OF THE BEND: the current cut it away. A tall raw earth cliff of ochre\n  #B08A4E with a HARD DARK BACKRUN RIM where the pour bit into it, at most 4 horizontal sediment\n  bands, and up to 3 pale root shapes #EFE7D2 left as bare paper hanging out of the face. The\n  water at its foot is #2E2519, deep, smooth and unbroken - no ripples at all, which is what\n  makes it look fast. NOTHING EVER DRIFTS AGAINST THIS BANK.\nRIGHT BANK, THE INSIDE OF THE BEND: the current dropped what it carried. A wide pale sediment\n  bloom #DCCBA4 with a crisp tideline where it dried, wet sand #A98F62 nearer the water, shallow\n  slow water over it. EVERYTHING THAT FLOATS DOWN COLLECTS ALONG THIS EDGE.\nTHE BAR NARROWS UPSTREAM and ends in a heap of coarse gravel - a nose about as high as the young\n  otter's shoulder when it stands on top. Past that heap the water runs hard against the cliff\n  with no dry ground between, so nobody can go further.\nBURROW HOLES: round holes in the cliff face, fist-sized. Three or four low down near the start of\n  the bend, then fewer as the cliff loses height going upstream, then none.\nWILLOWS: a few trees leaning out over the water from the top of the cliff, 6 poured strokes each,\n  0 leaves drawn. The LAST one stands just past the gravel nose and nothing grows beyond it.\n🔴 THE SECOND BEND: far upstream the river turns again, and what is round that turn is hidden by\n  the cliff's edge and the last willows. IT IS HIDDEN BY LAND, NEVER BY MIST. Nothing is ever\n  drawn past it.\nSHEET: a wide view of the whole bend from downstream / the same from the gravel nose looking\n  upstream / a close plate of the cliff face with holes and roots / a close plate of the wet\n  sand with a tideline and drifted twigs. LEFT IS ALWAYS THE CLIFF, RIGHT IS ALWAYS THE BAR - do\n  not mirror this on any page. No text or numerals. SCENE token: RiverBend.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - OtterYoung   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\nSame make: 2-3 poured shapes for the body on warm oatmeal rough paper, bare paper left inside\nfor the light. NO DRAWN CONTOUR. Do not render it finely just because it is alone on the sheet.\n\nBODY: a young otter, warm dark brown #6A5442, one long poured shape from nose to tail with a\n  paler poured belly; the tail is thick at the root and tapers, and it is about as long as the\n  body. Ears are two small notches in the silhouette. Feet are dark with webbed toes.\nFACE: broad flat muzzle, whiskers as a few pale gaps in the pour. EACH EYE IS A GAP OF BARE\n  PAPER left inside the dark head shape, with a separate short dark shape above it for a brow -\n  the brows are the acting and must lift, pull together and tilt. No painted-in eye, no\n  catchlight, no drawn hairs.\nWET AND DRY: wet, the poured shape is darker and the silhouette runs to points along the flanks;\n  dry, the same shape is paler and its edge is ragged and spiky. Matte - wet is value, never\n  shine.\nFIXED GRADE: four legs. It rises onto its haunches ONLY when its forepaws are working, and it\n  never stands or walks like a person. THE ARMPIT CARRY: things are clamped high under one\n  upper arm with the elbow pressed to the ribs, leaving both forepaws free - show this clearly.\nSHEET: four-legged in shallow water, one forepaw plunged in to the elbow / sitting back on the\n  haunches working with both forepaws, something clamped in one armpit / flat on the belly with\n  all four legs pushing, chin up / braced on the hind legs, one forepaw gripping and the arm\n  pulled taut / sitting curled on gravel, back rounded, tail laid out long; plus two close-ups -\n  astonished (brows high, eye gaps wide) and quietly pleased (eye gaps narrowed to slits).\nPlain paper, no scenery. SCENE token: OtterYoung.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - ReedBall   (bake THIRD, attach as @image3 - two states)\n\nThe lost ball, in the same poured medium. Two plates, same object.\n\nSTATE A - WEED WRAPPED: a fist-sized bundle with dark weed green #37502F wound round and round\n  it, strands trailing loose and wet. Nothing of what is underneath shows except one small\n  broken patch. In a row of drifted twigs and shells it is the only dark green thing.\nSTATE B - UNWRAPPED: a ball plaited from reeds, FADED GOLD #C9A961, and 🔴 three things are on\n  it and must be legible in one look. 1) One side carries a run of small scooped tooth marks in\n  a curved row, spaced exactly like a young otter's teeth. 2) A dried cord is wound round it\n  THREE TIMES, crooked, uneven, and finished in a lumpy clumsy knot - plainly not tied by a\n  grown-up. 3) One face of it is rubbed smooth and shiny where it was always held, paler than\n  the rest, with the plaiting worn flat.\nHALF STATE: the same ball with the green peeled down one side so both colours are on it at once,\n  the peeled strands hanging.\nSCALE PLATE: the ball beside OtterYoung's head AT THE SAME DEPTH - it is about HALF the height\n  of the head. And a carry plate: the ball clamped high in the armpit with the elbow down and\n  both forepaws free.\nPlain paper, no scenery, no text or numerals. SCENE token: ReedBall.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - Kingfisher   (bake FOURTH, attach as @image4)\n\nSame poured medium. This bird carries the only saturated colour in the book.\n\nBODY: a young kingfisher - back, crown and wings KINGFISHER BLUE #2C6FA8, breast and belly\n  BELLY ORANGE #D2792F, a short white throat patch as bare paper. Heavy straight dark beak,\n  nearly as long as the head. Very short tail, tiny red-brown feet. Compact and round.\nFACE: the eye is a gap of bare paper with a small dark shape above it. No catchlight.\nFIXED GRADE: a bird throughout. It perches, hops, turns its beak toward whoever it is speaking\n  to, and flies short hops from hole to hole. IT NEVER GESTURES OR POINTS WITH A WING, never\n  holds anything with a wing, never lands on the otter.\nSCALE PLATE: Kingfisher beside OtterYoung's forepaw AT THE SAME DEPTH - the whole bird is about\n  the size of that forepaw. Perspective must never flip this on any page.\n🔴 ITS BLUE AND ORANGE MUST NOT TINT THE WATER, THE CLIFF, THE SAND OR THE AIR - no reflection\n  in the river, no coloured light, no glow. The rest of the book is mud, ochre and sand.\nSHEET: standing in a round hole in an earth bank with the body half out and the neck stretched /\n  caught mid-hop between two holes, feet off the perch, wings briefly open / hunched on the very\n  tip of a thin drooping willow branch, head tilted; plus one close-up of the head with the beak\n  turned to speak.\nPlain paper, no scenery. SCENE token: Kingfisher."
