   "CHARACTER SHEET - LambTide   (bake FIRST, before any scene)\n\nSame make as the book: flat cut planes with crisp edges, zero shading, on a plain SILVER #C3CBCD\nground. Do not model this animal just because there is no background behind it.\n\nFACE: a lamb's head as one cream-white #F1ECE0 plane with a slightly darker muzzle shape cut into\n  it. 🔴 The eye is ONE small dark almond and there is NO eyebrow and no mouth line except when it\n  calls, and then the mouth is one small dark oval. The face never carries the feeling.\nFLEECE: white #F1ECE0, one plane, its outline cut in small soft bumps so the silhouette reads as\n  wool WITHOUT any drawn curls, texture or fibre inside it. 🔴 Zero strands. Ears are two separate\n  small planes that swing; legs are four thin dark #46525A planes; hooves are two-part.\n🔴 THE POSTURE LADDER IS THE ACTING - draw these four at the same size in a row: STRETCHED (running\n  uphill, body long, all four legs thrown out, tail up) · PLANTED (four hooves gathered close\n  together on a small plate, body upright) · FOLDED (body compressed and rounded, two forehooves\n  brought together on one spot, head down, ears dropped) · SITTING (hind legs folded, one forehoof\n  raised and pointing sideways). A child must read tired, brave and small from the outline alone.\n🔴 WET NEVER MEANS SHINE: when the lamb comes out of the water its legs are simply the darker rock\n  plane up to the ankle, cut with a hard edge, and drops are three small separate ovals. No gloss.\nSHEET: also one head-and-neck close-up looking straight up, and one back view. Plain ground, no\n  scenery. 0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: LambTide.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - OldCormorant   (bake SECOND)\n\nSame make and same cut edges as LambTide - attach the approved LambTide sheet so the two are made\nby one hand and the size between them is settled. Plain SILVER ground. One colour only: #16191D.\n\nBUILD: a long low bird - a single black plane for the body, a long neck line, a heavy hooked bill,\n  and two big wing planes that open wide and flat. 🔴 The whole animal reads as one angular black\n  line, the exact opposite of the lamb's soft white mass; at thumbnail size the two must never be\n  confused. Feet are two small dark planes with three toes each.\nFACE: the eye is one small pale almond cut out of the black head and nothing else. 🔴 No eyebrow,\n  no expression, and no red anywhere on this bird, including the eye - the pale cut and a small\n  white cheek shape are what make it readable. Worn plumage = at most 5 slightly paler cut notches\n  along the wing edge, never feather drawing.\n🔴 SCALE PLATE: the cormorant beside LambTide at THE SAME DEPTH - the bird is about as big as the\n  lamb's head. Two pages put their faces level with each other and perspective must not flip that.\nSHEET: perched with both wings spread wide and flat to dry, head turned to one side / wings half\n  folded, head turned / wings completely folded, neck drawn down into the body, sitting round and\n  low / head-and-bill close-up with the bill slightly open. 🔴 There is no pose in which it points,\n  reaches, beckons or shrugs. Plain ground. 0 lettering. SCENE token: OldCormorant.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - TideSet   (bake THIRD - 🔴 the ruler of the book, and the real work of this sheet)\n\nThe causeway and the lighthouse rock, empty of animals, in flat planes with crisp edges and zero\nshading. Colours: SILVER #C3CBCD, WATER #6F8794, DRY PALE #E0D6C0, ROCK #46525A, WHITE #F1ECE0,\nWEED GREEN #4F7A45.\n\nTHE PLACE: a straight causeway of wet flats running from a low green dyke with a FEW ROOF SHAPES on\n  it at one end, to a big layered black rock at the other with a white lighthouse standing on it.\n  The sky and the flats are the same SILVER, so the path reads as a strip of sky lying on the\n  ground. 🔴 Draw the whole thing once from the side, wide.\n🔴 THE WATER LADDER - the same view five times in a row, changing ONLY the area of the water plane:\n  A · a thin water band far off at one edge, and the runnels across the flats are hairline WATER\n    threads. The flats are otherwise unbroken SILVER with 0 prints on them.\n  B · the threads have thickened into bands; scattered small water shapes lie about the flats.\n  C · a single thin sheet of WATER covers the whole path - the path is gone, and there are 0\n    prints anywhere, because the marks went with it.\n  D · WATER is three quarters of the frame, and inside it lie TWO DARK ROCK-COLOURED SHAPES with\n    hard edges and nothing over them: a long one (a submerged sand ridge) and a small flat one (a\n    submerged rock). 🔴 This is how \"it is under water\" is drawn - no ripple, no wobble, no shine.\n  E · WATER fills everything but the rock, and it STOPS at the lower edge of the green band.\n  Put A and E side by side and check that the water edge is as crisp in both.\n🔴 THE DRY LADDER - three shapes in DRY PALE, side by side at the same scale, so the shrinking is\n  one glance: a LONG BAND about three lamb-lengths · a FLAT PLATE exactly the size of four hooves\n  set close together · a HANDSPAN.\n🔴 THE GREEN BAND, close up: the layered black rock with one WEED GREEN band round its middle;\n  below the band the rock is the dark wet plane, above it the rock is DRY PALE. The band is the\n  only green in the book. Show a lamb outline standing well above it, with a wide stretch of dry\n  rock between the two.\nSCALE: the lighthouse is ten times the lamb's height; the rock is twice it. 0 lettering, 0\n  numerals, 0 name plates on the lighthouse. SCENE token: TideSet."
