   "CHARACTER SHEET - FoxPost   (bake FIRST, before any scene)\n\nSame make as the book: a contour PRESSED into blue-grey board #7C8894 with graphite #2B3138 in the\ngrooves, the warm #D2812C stroked inside the shape, chalk #DCE2E6 only where snow has landed on\ntop. No white paint anywhere. Do not render this animal smoothly because there is no background.\n\nFACE: a fox cub - long narrow muzzle, big triangular ears, a small dark nose pressed as one wedge.\n  A drawn eye with a SEPARATE pressed brow above it; the brows are the acting and must lift, pull\n  together and tilt unevenly. Whiskers 3 pressed strokes a side.\nCOAT: the warm stroked in short marks, denser along the back and thin on the belly; ear insides and\n  lower legs darker graphite. 🔴 It is the third and smallest of the book's three warm things, and\n  it is never as saturated as the postbox plate.\n🔴 THE MOUTH IS OCCUPIED ON EVERY PAGE - draw the cub with a BLANK envelope held crossways in its\n  jaws, and 🔴 draw the envelope twice: INSIDE THE LAMP - the warm rubbed thin across it so it reads\n  cream. OUT ON THE SNOW - bare board with ONE pressed outline, so it is nearly invisible against\n  the field. Same object, two states, and the second one is why page 6 works.\n🔴 THE SNOW LOAD LADDER (three figures in a row, same pose, same size): CLEAN - board and warm, no\n  chalk. HALF - chalk laid over the back, shoulders and the tops of both ears. LOADED - chalk right\n  over the back, head, ear tops and brow, so about half the animal has gone pale. 🔴 The chalk is\n  laid ON TOP and the pressed contour still shows through it.\nSHEET: walking with one forepaw lifted and the mouth shut on the envelope / sunk to the chest with\n  the forelegs gone under and the hind legs kicking / flat on its back, four legs up, muzzle up /\n  standing with the mouth wide open calling, the envelope just leaving it / sitting back on the\n  hind legs, digging with both forepaws / sitting down small with the shoulders dropped / standing\n  right up on the hind legs, one forepaw pushing something upward. Plain board. 0 lettering.\nSCENE token: FoxPost.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - MotherFox   (bake SECOND, short)\n\nSame make, same board and the same pressed contour as FoxPost - attach the approved FoxPost sheet.\n🔴 She is in only two pages of the book and she carries the landing, so only two things matter.\n\nBUILD: the same animal fully grown - longer muzzle, deeper chest, a heavier tail, the eyes narrower\n  and the brows almost still. The warm on her coat is the same pigment stroked the same way.\n🔴 HER FOOTPRINT IS TWICE THE CUB'S. Draw the two prints side by side, pressed into the board at the\n  same depth, and then draw HER PRINT SET DOWN OVER HIS so the small one sits inside the big one and\n  both rims are still readable. 🔴 That single drawing is the last page of the book and it must be\n  settled here, not invented in a scene.\nSHEET: standing in a doorway on four legs with the neck stretched down, passing something from her\n  mouth / walking forward with one forepaw just landing in a print, head up and eyes creased.\n  🔴 She never picks the cub up, never carries it and never leans over it. Four legs throughout -\n  no clothes, no fingers. Plain board. 0 lettering. SCENE token: MotherFox.",
