   "CHARACTER SHEET - HullPlates   (bake FIRST, before anything else - this book is these four shapes)\n\nThe four boat bottoms, as cut paper. 🔴 CUT ONE HULL SILHOUETTE AND USE IT FOUR TIMES: same\noutline, same size, same curve, no exceptions. What changes is only the paper mounted behind the\nopening. Lay all four on a plain teal ground #1E6068 in a row.\n\n1 BARNACLE HULL: the opening filled with heavily pebbled chip paper #E8E4DA - a hard raised\n  speckle edge to edge, no wood showing, lighter than the ground so the grain reads. A few\n  speckles break the outline. This paper must look ROUGH TO A FINGERTIP.\n2 MOSS HULL: filled with long-fibre mulberry paper #4C6B3C, torn not cut, LOOSE FIBRES HANGING\n  FREE below the hull line all leaning one way. It must look SLIPPERY AND MOVING.\n3 NEW-PAINT HULL: filled with machine-smooth coated stock #DCE5E3, absolutely no tooth, one clean\n  scissor edge, 0 marks inside. It must look SLICK, with nothing to hold on to.\n4 OWN HULL: filled with worn laid paper #6B5540 showing its laid lines, a few scattered barnacle\n  discs, one flat stain shape, and 🔴 A DARK IRON NAIL with the ORANGE NET hooked on it - a piece\n  of punched open-mesh paper #D2712C, one sheet thick, the mesh CUT as holes, never woven, never\n  string. One mesh square is torn open where a claw pulled out.\n🔴 ROW PLATE: the four hulls hanging along the quay wall in fixed left-to-right order - BARNACLE,\n  MOSS, NEW PAINT, OWN (own is the last boat at the far end). This order never flips. Above them,\n  four identical bows each with THE SAME painted eye motif, so nothing tells them apart from above.\n🔴 FAR PLATE: the same four seen from far below - ONE FLAT DARK SHAPE EACH #22323A, no pattern\n  paper legible at all. A pattern only shows when the crab is close enough to touch it.\nPlain ground, no animals, no text or numerals. SCENE token: HullPlates.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - CrabSmall   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\nCut paper, same make as the hulls - flat shapes with scissor edges, no outline, no shading, no\nsheen. It must look made of the same material as everything else.\n\nBODY: a small crab in low-chroma sand grey #A79482, the shell one flat rounded shape with a\n  slightly darker cut piece for the back edge. EIGHT LEGS, each a separate narrow cut strip with\n  two joints, and they hook and grip one at a time. TWO CLAWS, each a cut shape that opens and\n  closes - they GRIP, RUB and TAP and do nothing else. Small water beads = at most 4 pale discs.\nEYES: two eyestalks, each a thin strip with a small cut disc on top and a darker centre. 🔴 They\n  extend, retract and part sideways, and THEY NEVER MAKE AN EXPRESSION - the acting is the angle\n  of the stalks, the spread of the legs and the height of the claws.\n🔴 SCALE PLATE: the crab beside ONE barnacle disc at the same depth - the crab is three times the\n  barnacle. And beside the orange net piece - the net is exactly what two claws can hold at once.\nSHEET: hanging upside down under a plank, all eight legs hooked, both claws closed on a net /\n  falling free, legs splayed in all directions, one claw still reaching up / standing on sand with\n  the body low and both eyestalks reaching straight up / clinging to a round pile sideways, one\n  claw hooked into a groove, water running off the shell / pressed flat against a wall with both\n  claws hanging slack and the eyestalks laid sideways / two legs slipping wide apart on something\n  smooth while one claw rubs it.\nPlain ground, nothing in the claws except where stated. SCENE token: CrabSmall.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - OctopusYoung   (bake THIRD, attach as @image3)\n\nCut paper, same make as the crab.\n\nBODY: a young octopus in cool grey #6B6F78, the mantle one soft flat shape, EIGHT ARMS as tapering\n  cut strips that wrap a wooden pile. Suckers = small pale discs along the inner edge, at most 9 an\n  arm. One large eye, a cut disc with a dark horizontal centre, which rolls to look.\nFIXED GRADE: it stays stuck to the pile. It clings, rolls one eye, lifts an arm tip in the water\n  and speaks - 🔴 IT NEVER POINTS, never gestures, never indicates a direction, never leaves the\n  pile and never touches a boat. It is not a helper; it only answers.\n🔴 SCALE PLATE: OctopusYoung beside CrabSmall on the same pile AT THE SAME DEPTH - the octopus is\n  five times the crab. Perspective must never flip these two sizes on any page.\nSHEET: wrapped round a pile with the mantle half out of the water, one eye rolling toward a crab /\n  the same, with one arm tip dangling in the water and curling / seen only as one arm and the edge\n  of an eye at the frame's edge / settled low on the pile watching something far off, arms tight.\nPlain ground, no scenery. SCENE token: OctopusYoung."
