   "CHARACTER SHEET - DustPlate   (bake FIRST - this book is this one boundary)\n\nA test board, no characters. Show what FLOUR LOOKS LIKE when flour is UNDRAWN PAPER, on warm cream\n#EFE7D6 with thick black conte dust.\n\nA · THE BOUNDARY: a broad conte-drawn shape - say a table edge and a jar - crossed by a flour\n  boundary. On one side the strokes are full and heavy; at the boundary they STOP. 🔴 The boundary\n  is not a drawn line: it is where the dust was lifted away with a cloth, so it is soft, crumbly\n  and a little ragged, with a few loose grains beyond it. 🔴 Inside the white side there are ZERO\n  marks - 0 outline, 0 tone, 0 ghost of the jar. Bare paper only.\nB · COUNTING: one drawn animal paw beside one white paw. The white paw has NO contour at all - it\n  is a hole in the drawing, and it is legible only because the arm above it is still drawn. Show\n  three of these in a row so a child can count \"one, two, three\" with no shading to argue about.\nC · THE CLOUD LADDER: three puffs at the same scale - A FIST, A TORSO, A WHOLE ROOM. Each is\n  simply an area with no strokes in it, bounded by that crumbly lifted edge, with loose grains\n  flying off the rim. 🔴 None of them is painted, tinted or made lighter.\nD · 🔴 WET RETURN: a patch where water has been poured onto flour. The dry side is bare paper; from\n  the wet line inward, THE CONTE COMES BACK - heavy rubbed grey #857D71 going to near black,\n  wrinkling as it soaks. 🔴 Wetness = strokes returning, never a shine or a highlight.\nE · OCHRE SURVIVES: an ochre #C79A5A lump sitting inside a white area, its colour completely\n  unchanged, with a thin band of full-strength conte still round its base - the one thing the\n  flour cannot take.\nNo characters, no text or numerals. SCENE token: DustPlate.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - BakeryFlat   (bake SECOND - the room, on all twelve pages)\n\nThe workroom, empty of characters, in conte dust on cream paper. Its layout never changes.\n\nTHE PLACE: a heavy wooden table across the middle (2 edge strokes + 3 grain strokes, 0 knots), an\n  arched brick oven in the back wall (1 arch + at most 5 course lines), a shelf beside it with a\n  bread basket (4 loaf shapes, 0 scoring), ONE tall window at the RIGHT, an open flour sack at the\n  LEFT (1 shape + 3 fold strokes), a blue enamel bucket #2F6E9E under the table, and 🔴 THE OCHRE\n  DOUGH LUMP #C79A5A at the RIGHT END OF THE TABLE, wet and heavy. A wooden step-stool at the\n  table's left side. Nothing else exists in this book.\n🔴 THE WINDOW SHAFT: one slanting band of bare paper crossing the room from the window, and 🔴 IT\n  IS THE ONLY PLACE AIRBORNE GRAINS ARE EVER DRAWN - at most 20 small dots inside it, 0 outside it.\n🔴 THE OVEN MOUTH: the one other ochre in the book, the same ink as the dough. Draw the room three\n  ways: (i) fully drawn, every count above at full strength; (ii) with the whole room UNDRAWN\n  except the oven arch and the dough; (iii) 🔴 with the whole room undrawn INCLUDING THE OVEN, so\n  the ochre dough is the last coloured thing left.\n0 lettering, 0 numerals, 0 price cards, 0 shop signs. SCENE token: BakeryFlat.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - MoleKid   (bake THIRD)\n\nConte dust on cream paper, the same broad crumbly stroke as the room - no finer and no smoother.\n\nBODY: a young mole, small and round, standing on two legs, forepaws used as hands, big digging\n  claws on them, a short waistcoat. The coat is solid conte black #201C19 laid in broad strokes -\n  0 individual hairs. It is a quarter of the bear's height.\nFACE: a blunt PINK-PALE SNOUT left as bare paper with two nostril dots, tiny round eyes drawn as\n  small marks, and a SEPARATE SHORT BROW above each - the brows do all the acting. No catchlight.\n🔴 THE WHITE LADDER (four figures in a row, same pose, same size): (1) nothing white - all conte;\n  (2) BOTH FOREPAWS white, i.e. NO STROKES ON THEM AT ALL, legible only because the arms above are\n  drawn; (3) forepaws, nose tip and both ears white; (4) 🔴 THE WHOLE BODY UNDRAWN - the figure is\n  bare paper and the only marks left are the two small eyes. 🔴 At every step the white parts have\n  no contour and no faint tone. This ladder is the book.\nSHEET: standing on a stool with its chin on a table edge, both forepaws flat on it, on tiptoe /\n  one forepaw pressed down hard on its own snout, eyes screwed shut, chest swelling, other forepaw\n  gripping a table edge / thrown forward after a sneeze, both forepaws flung out, ears back, eyes\n  round / both forepaws clamped over its snout, elbows out, cheeks bulging, eyes rolled up / face\n  buried in an apron, only two ears out / hugging a bucket almost its own size and tipping it.\nPlain paper, no room. SCENE token: MoleKid.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - BearBaker   (bake FOURTH)\n\nSame conte, same hand as MoleKid - attach the approved MoleKid sheet.\n\nBODY: a big brown bear standing on two legs, forepaws as hands, a broad flat back and a heavy\n  belly, wearing ONE thing: a striped apron in bucket blue #2F6E9E over cream. The coat is broad\n  conte strokes, 0 hairs drawn. He is four times the mole's height.\nFACE: a broad blunt muzzle, a HEAVY BROW RIDGE that does the acting, and two small dark eyes.\n  🔴 WHITE STATE: when he is floured, draw him with NO STROKES ANYWHERE - no outline, no apron\n  stripes, no muzzle - and 🔴 ONLY THE TWO BLACK EYES REMAIN, blinking in bare paper. Draw that\n  figure on this sheet beside the fully drawn one, at the same size and pose, so the pair can be\n  compared.\n🔴 FIXED GRADE: he never scolds, never says \"be careful\" and never solves anything. His whole part\n  is standing there, laughing, blinking and finally lifting one foot. He does not sneeze.\nSHEET: tipping a wooden tub to pour flour, shoulders hunched, eyes only on the pouring / head\n  thrown back laughing, one forepaw on his belly / both forepaws raised uselessly, brows sloped,\n  mouth open / standing stiff with both forepaws hanging, all white but the eyes / both forepaws\n  under a bucket, steadying it / one foot lifted knee-high, looking down at his own sole, laughing.\nPlain paper, no room. SCENE token: BearBaker."
